  "worked_solution": "Recall that $f:X\\to Y$ is an *into* function if it is not a surjection. In other words, there exists an element $y$ in $Y$ such that it has no preimage in $X:$ \n\n$f:X\\to Y$ is an into function if $∃\\,y\\in Y$ such that $∀\\,x\\in X,$ we have $f(x)\\ne y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true.  Indeed,   there is an arrow from $10\\in X$ to $25\\in Y.$ So,  $f(10)=25.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the diagram, for every element $y\\in Y,$ we have an arrow from some element $x\\in X$ to it, which means that $f(x)=y.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since statement II is false, $f$ is *not* an *into* function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
